All right, wait.

137
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:37,240
Uh -huh. Oh, good.

138
00:07:37,640 --> 00:07:39,800
Boner. Good. Yeah, great.

139
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:42,220
Uh -huh. Yeah, okay.

140
00:07:42,600 --> 00:07:43,340
All right, thanks a lot.

141
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:48,100
Bye -bye. Dad!

142
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:49,780
Hey, can we talk?

143
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:56,220
Sure. Uh, look, I know that I kind of got upset, uh, this morning when you said I

144
00:07:56,220 --> 00:07:57,460
couldn't take the karate lessons.

145
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:03,500
Uh -huh. And I know that in the past I may have used these activities to meet girls.

146
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:07,840
Uh -huh. But, you know, I'm really glad you put your foot down about these karate

147
00:08:07,840 --> 00:08:11,960
lessons, Dad, because it forced me to look deeper into myself

148
00:08:12,760 --> 00:08:17,020
and ask, Mike, why do you want to take these lessons?

149
00:08:17,640 --> 00:08:19,960
Uh -huh. And the answer came to me, Dad.

150
00:08:21,040 --> 00:08:23,140
Mike, you want to take karate

151
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:25,900
because it develops discipline.

152
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:32,520
Oh. Not only physical discipline, Dad, but spiritual discipline as well.

153
00:08:33,260 --> 00:08:34,600
That's important to you, is it?

154
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:35,980
Oh, yeah, Dad.

155
00:08:36,860 --> 00:08:42,860
I mean, hey, without discipline, Each one of us is like a rudderless craft in a sea

156
00:08:42,860 --> 00:08:44,100
of conflicting desires.

157
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:49,280
And you know what, Dad?

158
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:53,060
I need the rudder that karate will give me.

159
00:08:54,580 --> 00:09:01,360
I see. So what you're saying is that you're taking karate to help you achieve the

160
00:09:01,360 --> 00:09:04,440
spiritual state of Don Ho.

161
00:09:06,340 --> 00:09:07,680
Right. Don Ho.

162
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:16,660
Mike, Don Ho is the Hawaiian singer who recorded Tiny Bubbles.

163
00:09:19,140 --> 00:09:21,220
Right. Very spiritual guy.

164
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:24,040
You ain't nothing but a hound dog

165
00:09:24,740 --> 00:09:31,420
Cryin' all the time You ain't nothing but a hound dog Cryin' all the time Well, you

166
00:09:31,420 --> 00:09:34,420
ain't never called a rabbit And you ain't no friend of mine

167
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:39,200
Get it! He's after 46 minutes now.

168
00:09:39,220 --> 00:09:40,200
We're gonna do it.

169
00:09:40,680 --> 00:09:42,100
Through what? Throw out his hip?

170
00:09:43,460 --> 00:09:47,320
No, break the world's record for the most consecutive hours of imitating Elvis.

171
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,500
I take it you're going for endurance here, not accuracy.

172
00:09:52,660 --> 00:09:54,620
How much longer before he gets in the book?

173
00:09:54,920 --> 00:09:56,200
What time is it now?

174
00:09:56,400 --> 00:09:59,080
1.30? A week from Thursday?

175
00:10:01,340 --> 00:10:06,480
Carol. Ben. I'm sure Elvis would be touched.

176
00:10:06,480 --> 00:10:07,660
Crying all the time.

177
00:10:07,700 --> 00:10:10,520
You can't keep this up for another 12 days.

178
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:12,660
Crying all the time.

179
00:10:13,380 --> 00:10:14,820
The king is dead.

180
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:22,420
I'm sorry. How do you feel?

181
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:24,620
I'm all shook up.

182
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:29,660
Uh -uh. Uh, yeah, yeah.

183
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:30,860
I'm all shook up.

184
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:36,980
Oh, so, did you finish scoring our test yet?

185
00:10:37,020 --> 00:10:38,420
Yep. Well, what's it say?

186
00:10:38,500 --> 00:10:39,020
What's it say?

187
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:40,900
Well, for one thing, it says you're impatient.

188
00:10:41,380 --> 00:10:42,680
Oh, come on, Jason.

189
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:44,000
Are we compatible or not?

190
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:44,960
I am, you're not.

191
00:10:45,460 --> 00:10:48,720
Darn. Now, go ahead and see for yourself.

192
00:10:48,760 --> 00:10:50,560
We got an excellent score.

193
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:52,680
Mm -hmm. Agreed on just about everything.

194
00:10:53,300 --> 00:10:54,840
Especially the smutty question.

195
00:10:56,400 --> 00:10:58,540
Oh, here's one we disagreed on.

196
00:10:58,700 --> 00:11:00,380
What is your favorite hobby?

197
00:11:00,600 --> 00:11:00,840
Oh, that's a good question.

198
00:11:00,840 --> 00:11:02,340
Oh, Jason. What?

199
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:05,680
I said gardening and you said collecting baseball cards.

200
00:11:05,920 --> 00:11:08,320
Oh. How can we go on with this rift in our marriage?

201
00:11:08,420 --> 00:11:12,660
It's impossible. We'll have to sell the house and divide the kids.

202
00:11:13,680 --> 00:11:14,680
You take Mike.

203
00:11:16,940 --> 00:11:19,200
Uh -oh, here's another one you screwed up.

204
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:21,900
What side of the bed do you sleep on?

205
00:11:22,200 --> 00:11:24,420
I said the left and you said the left.

206
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:25,440
Well, that's right.

207
00:11:25,900 --> 00:11:28,500
You take up most of the bed and I sleep on what's left.

208
00:11:30,780 --> 00:11:34,200
Oh, no. Here's another one you botched up.

209
00:11:34,300 --> 00:11:35,880
Jason, you should be more careful.

210
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:38,560
Would you ever keep anything from your spouse?

211
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:40,440
What did I say?

212
00:11:41,340 --> 00:11:42,720
You said yes.

213
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:46,000
Well, that's what I meant.

214
00:11:47,260 --> 00:11:57,120
What? What do you mean you'd keep things from me?

215
00:11:57,640 --> 00:11:59,220
That's like saying that you'd lie to me.

216
00:11:59,220 --> 00:12:01,240
No, no, you're twisting my answer.

217
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:05,620
The question simply asks, would you ever keep anything from your spouse?

218
00:12:06,120 --> 00:12:07,520
Anything's a big word.

219
00:12:08,220 --> 00:12:10,060
It means anything.

220
00:12:12,220 --> 00:12:14,160
Well, thanks for clearing that up.

221
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:16,620
Now will you tell me what liar means?

222
00:12:17,080 --> 00:12:18,400
Oh, come on, Maggie.

223
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:20,400
What are you making such a big deal of this for?

224
00:12:20,700 --> 00:12:22,220
I've always been honest with you.

225
00:12:22,860 --> 00:12:28,460
Oh? Yes. Remember when somebody tried to mix cement in your blender?

226
00:12:30,600 --> 00:12:34,300
I stepped forward right away and said, Ben did it.

227
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:38,280
Jason, you think this is funny, don't you?

228
00:12:38,380 --> 00:12:39,700
No, honey, I'm just saying...

229
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:41,160
Well, I'll tell you what I remember.

230
00:12:41,680 --> 00:12:43,540
I remember the Mattapoiset Inn.

231
00:12:44,140 --> 00:12:45,620
The Mattapoiset... Yes.

232
00:12:45,940 --> 00:12:47,060
Where we had our honeymoon.

233
00:12:47,120 --> 00:12:51,200
Yes. Now, remember that first night, and remember in front of the fireplace when we

234
00:12:51,200 --> 00:12:53,940
sat... Honey, I remember what we did in front of the fireplace.

235
00:12:54,980 --> 00:12:56,600
Remember what you said.

236
00:12:56,820 --> 00:12:58,940
Yes. I said, let's try that again.

237
00:13:01,040 --> 00:13:02,700
Without the ice cubes.

238
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:09,200
You also said that total honesty is essential to a happy marriage.

239
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:11,820
I said that after we were married?

240
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:17,360
Okay, okay, I said that.

241
00:13:17,360 --> 00:13:19,420
But we're more mature now.

242
00:13:19,920 --> 00:13:22,060
So now we lie like rugs.

243
00:13:23,500 --> 00:13:27,600
No, I'm only saying that there are some things that I might not volunteer.

244
00:13:28,460 --> 00:13:30,300
Well, like what things?

245
00:13:30,500 --> 00:13:31,340
Give me an example.

246
00:13:31,820 --> 00:13:33,200
An example? Uh -huh.

247
00:13:33,980 --> 00:13:35,600
Two trains traveling from opposite directions.

248
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:37,560
Jason. Train A moving 85 miles an hour.

249
00:13:37,560 --> 00:13:39,340
I'm an example from our lives.

250
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:40,420
Well, you didn't let me finish.

251
00:13:40,540 --> 00:13:41,440
We were on train B.

252
00:13:41,680 --> 00:13:43,600
Damn it. Be serious.

253
00:13:44,540 --> 00:13:46,480
Oh, you want a serious example?

254
00:13:46,600 --> 00:13:54,100
Yes. Okay. Here's something I didn't tell you because I thought it might upset you.

255
00:13:54,560 --> 00:13:56,800
I will not be upset.

256
00:13:59,040 --> 00:14:03,040
A couple weeks ago, I ran into Allison Van Dyke at the dry cleaners.

257
00:14:03,620 --> 00:14:04,600
Your old girlfriend?

258
